===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# The [[terminal]] point of something in space or time.
#* {{RQ:Grahame Wind in the Willows|passage=they followed him... into a sort of a central hall; out of which they could dimly see other long tunnel-like passages branching, passages mysterious and without apparent '''end'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=4|passage=I told him about everything I could think of; and what I couldn't think of he did. He asked about six questions during my yarn, but every question had a point to it. At the '''end''' he bowed and thanked me once more. As a thanker he was main-truck high; I never see anybody so polite.}}
#: {{ux|en|At the '''end''' of the road, turn left.}}
#: {{ux|en|At the '''end''' of the story, the main characters fall in love.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} The cessation of an effort, activity, state, or motion.
#: {{ux|en|Is there no '''end''' to this madness?}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1986|title=The Army Communicator|volume=11|issue=2|page=46
|passage=The new electronic switching system means expanded service and an '''end''' to irritances such as cross-talk, line-hum, and being cut off in mid-conversation.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} {{1|death}}.
#: {{ux|en|He met a terrible '''end''' in the jungle.}}
#: {{ux|en|I hope the '''end''' comes quickly.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 3|II|i|text=Confound your hidden falsehood, and award / Either of you to be the other's '''end'''.}}
#* '''1732''', {{w|Alexander Pope}}, (epitaph) On Mr. Gay, in Westminster Abbey:
#*: A safe companion and an easy friend / Unblamed through life, lamented in thy '''end'''.
# The most extreme point of an object, especially one that is longer than it is wide.
#: {{ux|en|Hold the string at both '''ends'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|My father always sat at the '''end''' of the table nearest the kitchen.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Psalms|22|27|passage=All the '''ends''' of the woꝛld ſhall remember, and turne vnto the {{smallcaps|Lord}}: and all the kinreds of the nations ſhall woꝛſhip befoꝛe thee.}}
# [[result|Result]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Julius Caesar|V||text=O that a man might know / The '''end''' of this day's business ere it come!}}
#* '''1876''', Great Britain. Public Record Office, John Sherren Brewer, Robert Henry Brodie, James Gairdner, ''Letters and Papers, Foreign and Domestic, of the Reign of Henry VIII'' (volume 4, issue 3, part 2, page 3154)
#*: The '''end''' was that he was thought an archfool.
# {{senseid|en|purpose}} A [[purpose]], [[goal]], or [[aim]].
#: {{ux|en|For what '''end''' should I toil?}}
#: {{ux|en|The '''end''' of our club is to advance conversation and friendship.}}
#: {{syn|en|purpose}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Aureng-zebe|act=3|text=But, losing her, the '''End''' of Living lose.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1825|author=w:Samuel Taylor Coleridge|title=Aids to Reflection in the Formation of a Manly Character|section=Aphorism VI, page 146
|passage=When every man is his own '''end''', all things will come to a bad end.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1946|author=w:Bertrand Russell|title=History of Western Philosophy|section=I.21
|passage=There is a long argument to prove that foreign conquest is not the '''end''' of the State, showing that many people took the imperialist view.}}
# {{lb|en|cricket}} One of the two parts of the [[ground]] used as a [[descriptive]] [[name]] for [[half]] of the ground.
#: {{ux|en|The Pavillion '''End'''}}
# {{lb|en|American football}} The position at the end of either the offensive or defensive [[line]], a [[tight end]], a [[split end]], a [[defensive end]].
#* {{RQ:Fitzgerald Great Gatsby|year=1953|text=Her husband, among various physical accomplishments, had been one of the most powerful '''ends''' that ever played football at New Haven {{...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|curling}} A period of play in which each team throws eight rocks, two per player, in alternating fashion.
# {{lb|en|mathematics}} An [[ideal]] [[point]] of a [[graph]] or other [[complex]]. See {{w|End (graph theory)}}
# That which is left; a remnant; a fragment; a scrap.
#: {{ux|en|[[odds and ends|odds and '''ends''']]}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 3|I|iii|text=I clothe my naked villainy / With old odd '''ends''' stolen out of holy writ, / And seem a saint, when most I play the devil.}}
# One of the [[yarn]]s of the [[worsted]] [[warp]] in a [[Brussels carpet]].
# {{lb|en|in the plural|slang|AAVE}} [[money|Money]].
#: {{ux|en|Don't give them your '''ends'''. You jack that shit!}}

